  <remark>
    <title>Vector Operations in <m>xyz</m>-Space</title>

    <p>
      Vector operations in <m>xyz</m>-space work exactly the same as in the
      <m>xy</m>-plane. We do operations coordinate-wise.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>\vec{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>,
      <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}\right\rangle</m>, and
      <m>c</m> be a real number (scalar). Then
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{v}+\vec{u}=\left\langle v_{1}+u_{1}, v_{2}+u_{2},
            v_{3}+u_{3}\right\rangle</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{v}-\vec{u}=\left\langle v_{1}-u_{1}, v_{2}-u_{2},
            v_{3}-u_{3}\right\rangle</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>c \vec{v}=\left\langle c v_{1}, c v_{2}, c
            v_{3}\right\rangle</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Note: Geometrically, everything works the same. Adding/subtracting
      vectors can be done using the Triangle Rule. Scaling a vector changes
      its length and potentially flips the direction of the vector. It is
      harder to draw now, though...
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-02-ex-2-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\vec{v}=\langle 3,2,3\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{u}=\langle 1,2,1\rangle</m>, compute
        <m>2|\vec{v}-2\vec{u}|</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Working from the inside out, we first compute the vector
        <m>\vec{v}-2\vec{u}</m> coordinate-wise:
        <md>
          \vec{v}-2\vec{u} = \langle 3,2,3 \rangle - \langle 2,4,2 \rangle = \langle 1,-2,1 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Its magnitude is
        <md>
          |\vec{v}-2\vec{u}| = \sqrt{1^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1^{2}} = \sqrt{6}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        and hence <m>2|\vec{v}-2\vec{u}| = 2\sqrt{6}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Distance and Midpoint Formula</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given two points <m>P\left(x_{1}, y_{1}, z_{1}\right)</m> and
        <m>Q\left(x_{2}, y_{2}, z_{2}\right)</m> in <m>xyz</m>-space, we
        denote the line segment connecting <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> by
        <m>PQ</m>. Moreover, we have that
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The distance between the points <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> (the
              length of the line segment <m>PQ</m>) is given by
              <md>
                |P Q|=\sqrt{\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)^{2}+\left(y_{2}-y_{1}\right)^{2}+\left(z_{2}-z_{1}\right)^{2}}
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The midpoint on the line segment <m>PQ</m> is given by
              <md>
                \left(\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}}{2}, \frac{y_{1}+y_{2}}{2}, \frac{z_{1}+z_{2}}{2}\right)
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Note that the distance between <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> is simply
        <m>|\vec{P Q}|</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-02-midpoint">
    <caption>From Briggs, et al., Calculus Early Transcendentals (3e), Figure 13.33</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_01_578ccdf8ba98801b0a3bg-7.jpg" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Two points P and Q in xyz-space connected by a line segment, illustrating the midpoint formula.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>Two points <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> in <m>xyz</m>-space connected by a line segment, illustrating the midpoint formula.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <remark>
    <title>Magnitude</title>

    <p>
      Given a 3D vector
      <m>\vec{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>, then
      <m>|\vec{v}|=\sqrt{v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2}+v_{3}^{2}}</m>. As before,
      <m>\vec{v}</m> is a unit vector if <m>|\vec{v}|=1</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <title>Recall: Unit Vector in the Direction of <m>\vec{v}</m></title>

    <p>
      When working with a 3D vector <m>\vec{v}</m>, we can't define its
      direction by a single angle anymore (since <m>\vec{v}</m> is
      contained in infinitely many planes). Instead, we focus on the unit
      vector in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Given a nonzero vector
      <m>\vec{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>, the
      vector <m>\vec{u}=\frac{\vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|}</m> is a unit
      vector in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-02-ex-2-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\vec{v}=\langle 1,2,1\rangle</m>, find:
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) A unit vector in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) All vectors of length 3 parallel to <m>\vec{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The magnitude of <m>\vec{v}</m> is
        <m>|\vec{v}| = \sqrt{1^{2}+2^{2}+1^{2}} = \sqrt{6}</m>, so a unit
        vector in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m> is
        <md>
          \vec{u} = \frac{\vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\langle 1,2,1 \rangle = \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{2}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        A vector of length 3 parallel to <m>\vec{v}</m> points either in
        the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m> or in the direction opposite of
        <m>\vec{v}</m>, so the only two such vectors are
        <md>
          3\vec{u} = \left\langle \frac{3}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{6}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{3}{\sqrt{6}} \right\rangle \quad \text{and} \quad -3\vec{u} = \left\langle -\frac{3}{\sqrt{6}}, -\frac{6}{\sqrt{6}}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt{6}} \right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Coordinate Vectors</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        In <m>xyz</m>-space, the following vectors are called coordinate
        vectors:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\vec{i}=\langle 1,0,0\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\vec{j}=\langle 0,1,0\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\vec{k}=\langle 0,0,1\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Given a vector
        <m>\vec{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>, we
        can also write <m>\vec{v}</m> in the form
        <md>
          \vec{v}=v_{1} \vec{i}+v_{2} \vec{j}+v_{3} \vec{k}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <figure xml:id="Coordinate-Vectors-Animation-Web" component="web">
    <caption>Vector Addition</caption>
    <image source="images/GIF/L3-AnimatedExample.gif" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
            An animation that expresses a vector as the sum of its coordinate vectors.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <figure xml:id="Coordinate-Vectors-Animation-Print" component="print">
    <caption>For an animated version of this figure, see the web version of this figure at the <url href="https://kostaskydoniatis-lang.github.io/PreText-Calculus-III--test" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">online notes</url></caption>
    <image source="images/GIF/L3-AnimatedExample.jpg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
            An animation that expresses a vector as the sum of its coordinate vectors.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-02-ex-2-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that
        <m>\langle -2,3,1\rangle=-2 \vec{i}+3 \vec{j}+ \vec{k}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Writing each scaled coordinate vector in component form and adding
        coordinate-wise:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>-2\vec{i}+3\vec{j}+\vec{k} = -2 \langle 1,0,0\rangle + 3 \langle 0,1,0\rangle + \langle 0,0,1\rangle = \langle -2,0,0 \rangle + \langle 0,3,0 \rangle + \langle 0,0,1 \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>= \langle -2+0+0, 0+3+0, 0+0+1 \rangle = \langle -2,3,1 \rangle</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        as claimed.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
